  "statement": "Corporate boards that approve enterprise AI deployments rely on accounting tricks that disguise administrative overhead as productivity gains. Management points to internal pilot projects showing task-speed increases of thirty to forty percent in isolated software drafting tests. That speed hides the reality of error correction, system monitoring, and workload shifting onto surviving staff. When software output requires triple-checking by senior analysts, the enterprise has not gained efficiency. The enterprise has outsourced validation to human workers while the software vendor captures the licensing fee.\n\nSafetyists argue that corporate directors violate their fiduciary duty to preserve organizational stability by rushing unmanaged algorithms into core workflows. That argument correctly identifies the governance failure, but it treats the software as a rogue agent rather than a management tool. The real mechanism at work is labor displacement disguised as technological uplift. Historical automation waves in manufacturing followed this exact path. Management shed shop-floor staff, loaded extra tasks onto operators, and reported margin expansion while workers absorbed the physical strain.\n\nCorporate governance boards now deploy narrow compliance audits to deflect regulatory scrutiny over labor impacts. Current disclosure frameworks measured by the Securities and Exchange Commission evaluate model usage rates and policy adoption rather than worker displacement, burnout, or wage stagnation. Companies satisfy auditors with policy checkboxes while shedding headcount through quiet attrition. This compliance theater protects the software vendor and the board from liability while transferring operational friction directly to the cubicle. No boardroom metric measures the cost of an exhausted analyst fixing predictable machine errors at midnight.\n\nFederal labor disclosures under SEC rules must mandate genuine metrics before boards approve further software spending. Regulators should require public reporting on net headcount shifts, error-correction labor hours, and wage changes alongside any capital expenditure on enterprise software. The political coalition to back this mandate exists among labor unions and mid-level management burdened by administrative bloat. The opposition comes from venture-backed software firms and corporate boards protecting licensing margins. If enterprise AI deployment generates real productivity, why do companies need to hide the headcount reduction behind compliance theater?",
